SUPERFRAME PHASE -- can a wrong one be held?

  mfspec: the only thing pinning the phase was two pads in the S lane,
  4 apart, so the check only ever compared channels 4 apart and any
  pair differing in hook state admitted a wrong phase -- 56 of 64.

  mf8: F itself carries the phase.  At offset d the receiver expects
  11111110 and sees it rotated by d, which mismatches at 2 frames in
  8 for every d != 0.  Hook states cannot enter into it.

   offset   frames matching of 8   longest good run
        0                      8                 64
        1                      6                  6
        2                      6                  5
        3                      6                  4
        4                      6                  3
        5                      6                  4
        6                      6                  5
        7                      6                  6

  Every wrong offset tops out at 6 consecutive good frames, against
  the 32 acquisition needs.  Rejected deterministically, for every
  hook state, with no enumeration required -- but here it is anyway:
  wrong superframe phases admissible over all 64 hook states: 0

GUARD POSITION -- why 3 and 5, not 3 and 7

  False locks over all 64 hook states x 8 skews (512 per cell).  "one
  data card" is the F word in ONE seat, voice elsewhere -- legal under the
  slot rule, so it has to be 0 in every seat for the rule to mean anything.

  layout                        F word, every lane  one data card, worst seat
  3,5 = 1,0  (this spec)                   0 / 512                    0 / 512
  3,7 = 0,1  (regular)                     1 / 512                    1 / 512
  3,7 = 1,0  (mfspec values)             384 / 512                    1 / 512

  mfspec's values copy the F word at states 3 and 7, so they add nothing.
  Inverting them fixes that -- but 3 and 7 are half a superframe apart, and
  a half-superframe shift maps the pair onto itself, swapped.  With every
  channel off-hook the S lane has one zero and impersonates F; locking on
  it shifts the receiver by exactly 4, the guard then reads the next lane at
  states 7 and 3, and the F word there reads 0,1 -- which is what the
  inverted guard wants.  3 and 5 have no such symmetry.

SLOT RULES -- what a data card may sit next to

  The adversarial content is the F word (11111110) in one lane and a
  guard-satisfying pattern (bit 3 = 1, bit 5 = 0) in the next.

  layout  (V voice, D data, . silence)              false

  -- one data card, any seat, voice elsewhere:
    seat 1 = F word, rest voice                   0 / 512
    seat 2 = F word, rest voice                   0 / 512
    seat 3 = F word, rest voice                   0 / 512
    seat 4 = F word, rest voice                   0 / 512
    seat 5 = F word, rest voice                   0 / 512
    seat 6 = F word, rest voice                   0 / 512
    seat 1 = guard pattern, rest idle             0 / 512
    seat 2 = guard pattern, rest idle             0 / 512
    seat 3 = guard pattern, rest idle             0 / 512
    seat 4 = guard pattern, rest idle             0 / 512
    seat 5 = guard pattern, rest idle             0 / 512
    seat 6 = guard pattern, rest idle             0 / 512

  -- two ADJACENT data cards, worst content:
    seats 1,2 = word,guard; rest voice          128 / 512
    seats 2,3 = word,guard; rest voice          192 / 512
    seats 3,4 = word,guard; rest voice          256 / 512
    seats 4,5 = word,guard; rest voice          320 / 512
    seats 5,6 = word,guard; rest voice          384 / 512

  -- two NON-adjacent data cards, worst content:
    seats 1,3 = word,guard; rest voice            0 / 512
    seats 1,4 = word,guard; rest voice            0 / 512
    seats 1,6 = word,guard; rest voice            0 / 512
    seats 2,4 = word,guard; rest voice            0 / 512
    seats 2,6 = word,guard; rest voice            0 / 512
    seats 3,6 = word,guard; rest voice            0 / 512
    seats 4,6 = word,guard; rest voice            0 / 512

  -- random data in every seat (the ordinary data case):
    six data cards, random content                0 / 512

  THE RULE.  One data card is safe in any seat whatever it carries;
  two data cards are safe when they are not adjacent; two ADJACENT
  data cards are not safe, because a wrong alignment can put the F
  check on one and the guard on the other and neither is constrained.
  This is the same shape of rule framing-review.md 5 found for the
  4-bit word ("any seat, but never two adjacent") and spec.md 3.4
  avoided on the 16-bit frame by strapping a data card's A bit -- a
  bit this frame does not have, because signalling is sub-commutated.

  Note that ordinary random data in all six seats is safe: the hole
  needs the framing word specifically, not merely arbitrary data.

